\details{
\strong{FCA indices.} With raster data (\code{\link[=prep_data]{prep_data()}} with a \code{SpatRaster} as demand)
and an FCA specification (\code{\link[=spec_2sfca]{spec_2sfca()}}, \code{\link[=spec_e2sfca]{spec_e2sfca()}}, \code{\link[=spec_m2sfca]{spec_m2sfca()}},
\code{\link[=spec_3sfca]{spec_3sfca()}}, \code{\link[=spec_huff2sfca]{spec_huff2sfca()}}), \code{spax()} computes the index and returns
the same object as the matching one-shot (\code{\link[=spax_2sfca]{spax_2sfca()}}, \code{\link[=spax_e2sfca]{spax_e2sfca()}} and
the others): the \code{type}, \code{parameters} and accessibility layers are identical,
and \code{call} is the call of \code{spax()}. An index is computed, not fitted: \code{observed},
\code{starts}, \code{lower}, \code{upper} and \code{...} are refused. Group lists of \code{decay} and
\code{selection} and a \code{threshold} by group are matched to the demand groups of the data
by name and put in their order.

\strong{FCA indices on numeric data.} With numeric data (demand, supply and a travel
matrix) an FCA specification gives the index at the active origins, computed by the
same engine and the same rules (the cells that reach no facility are \code{NA}, and the
overall layer of demand groups is the same). The result is a \code{spax_access_values}, a
list with \code{access}, a matrix with one row per active origin and one column per layer,
not a raster (see \link{spax_access_values-methods}). Numeric data has one supply
measure, \code{"supply"}, so there is one measure, and an origin with zero or unknown
demand has no row. A raster \code{sigma} (a variable catchment) is refused: "a raster
sigma needs raster data".

\strong{Allocation models on raster data.} With raster data and an allocation model
(\code{\link[=spec_clm]{spec_clm()}}, \code{\link[=spec_huff]{spec_huff()}}), \code{spax()} first converts the data to origins, on each
call and without keeping anything: the cells with finite, positive demand are the
origins, their IDs are the cell numbers (\code{"843"}) and each has its travel to every
facility; a missing or infinite travel time is an absent pair, and a cell that reaches
no facility stays an origin whose demand goes to the outside option. \code{\link[=obs_totals]{obs_totals()}}
works as it is, and \code{\link[=obs_flows]{obs_flows()}} is keyed by cell number: counts at cells that are not
origins are dropped with a message. The model then runs as on numeric data, so every
step function accepts the result, and fits to the same data can be compared. See
\code{\link[=prep_data]{prep_data()}} for the conversion and its memory.

\strong{Fit mode.} Some parameter is \code{"fit"}. \code{observed} is needed. The counts
are aligned with the data (see \code{\link[=obs_flows]{obs_flows()}}), the model is bound to the
data and \code{\link[=fit_allocation]{fit_allocation()}} fits it. The loss is set by the observation and
the output it is compared with by its kind, so \code{output}, \code{loss} and
\code{loss_args} are refused in \code{...}. Starts, bounds and optimizer settings come
from the data when they are not given: \code{starts}, \code{lower} and \code{upper} are
optional (see the default search in \code{\link[=fit_allocation]{fit_allocation()}}). \code{spax()} always
passes analytic gradients and the strict optimizer settings
\code{control = list(maxit = 300, factr = 1e3, pgtol = 1e-2)}, also when you give
\code{starts}, unless \code{...} gives \code{gradient} or \code{control} itself. The result does
not solve the model again: it reads the selected fit. A search in which no
start converged gives a warning and a result with no estimates;
\code{summary()} shows the starts and \code{as.data.frame(type = "starts")} their table.

\strong{Outside counts.} An observation that declares outside counts (the
\code{outside} argument of \code{\link[=obs_flows]{obs_flows()}} and \code{\link[=obs_totals]{obs_totals()}}) is fitted with the
outputs \code{"flow_outside"}, \code{"utilization_outside"} or
\code{"utilization_outside_by_group"} of \code{\link[=fit_allocation]{fit_allocation()}}: the observed counts
with the outside count as one more column, element or row named
\code{"(outside)"}, and the Poisson loss. The model needs an outside option
(\code{\link[=spec_clm]{spec_clm()}} with an outside weight above 0 somewhere): with a model without
one (\code{\link[=spec_huff]{spec_huff()}}, or \code{outside = 0} everywhere) \code{spax()} stops with "outside
